<Chapter id="glossary">
<h2>Glossary</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Battle.net platform</dt>
    <dd>The complete modern account and game-service system. It includes web
    authentication, Front, GameUtilities, Sunken, and game-native services.</dd>

    <dt>Front WebSocket</dt>
    <dd>The RFC 6455 transport with subprotocol <code>v1.rpc.battle.net</code>. One
    binary message contains one complete Front RPC message.</dd>

    <dt>Cached web credential</dt>
    <dd>An opaque value from AuthenticationServer method 8. A later LogonRequest can
    use this value. The web challenge returns a separate short-lived credential.</dd>

    <dt>Front</dt>
    <dd>The regional SC2 account and session connection. It carries protobuf RPC
    over WebSocket and supplies the native handoff.</dd>

    <dt>Sunken</dt>
    <dd>The SC2-native endpoint that accepts Resume authentication and then carries
    protected, bit-packed service records.</dd>

    <dt>GameUtilities</dt>
    <dd>A protobuf service available through Front. Its <code>ProcessClientRequest</code> method performs
    the SC2-specific Front-to-Sunken handoff on this path.</dd>

    <dt>Protobuf descriptor</dt>
    <dd>Schema metadata for a Front message or service. Service names and methods
    are recoverable from standard protobuf descriptors embedded in the client.</dd>

    <dt>BSN metadata</dt>
    <dd>Version-7 schema metadata embedded in the SC2 executable. A numeric type ID
    selects a structural record and an optional naming record. Front uses separate
    protobuf descriptors.</dd>

    <dt>Frame</dt>
    <dd>The explicitly named Sunken <code>Connection/13 MessageFrame</code> type.
    Front RPC messages and Sunken stream records use their own boundary terms.</dd>

    <dt>Packet</dt>
    <dd>An informal term that may refer to a <i>Front RPC message</i>, <i>Sunken record</i>, or
    <i>TCP segment</i>. Each term identifies a different boundary.</dd>

    <dt>Record</dt>
    <dd>One Sunken stream unit. A service record contains an 11-bit route, a
    schema-delimited payload, and final padding. A command response contains a
    seven-bit prefix and a nine-bit result.</dd>

    <dt>Schema-delimited</dt>
    <dd>A service-record boundary that the payload schema sets. Local counts,
    selectors, and presence bits set nested sizes. The end of the top-level value
    is the logical record end.</dd>

    <dt>Route</dt>
    <dd>The native wire tuple <code>(direction, slot, command)</code>. Direction
    matters because one command number can name different request and notify
    types. A static build table supplies the readable service label.</dd>

    <dt>Service hash</dt>
    <dd>A 32-bit FNV-1a hash of a full protobuf service name. It is included in
    every Front request, including the initial Connect.</dd>

    <dt>Front RPC token</dt>
    <dd>A protobuf-header value that identifies a related RPC call. Chat requests
    use a separate application-level <code>m_token</code>.</dd>

    <dt>Authentication module ID</dt>
    <dd>The 40-byte ID in an Auth/2 <code>ModuleInput</code>. It contains an
    eight-byte usage prefix and a 32-byte module identity. The ID selects the module
    operation. Output cardinality depends on the selected modules. See the
    <a href="#auth-module-id">layout and known IDs</a>.</dd>

    <dt>Service slot</dt>
    <dd>A four-bit number selecting a Sunken service. Slot constants are
    build-specific. FourCC values and chat-channel indexes occupy separate fields.</dd>

    <dt>Client channel index</dt>
    <dd>A three-bit Chat field that selects a local chat channel. Its wire range is
    0 through 6. The service grants a maximum of six concurrent channels. A client
    must use a maximum of six channels. The routing header carries the service slot
    separately.</dd>

    <dt>Front account entity ID</dt>
    <dd>A 128-bit protobuf entity identifying the Battle.net platform account. It
    maps to a separate 32-bit account ID in native social services.</dd>

    <dt>Front game-account entity ID</dt>
    <dd>A 128-bit protobuf entity identifying one game account or license under a
    Battle.net account. The selected SC2 game account is passed to Game Utilities.</dd>

    <dt>Toon handle</dt>
    <dd>A stable native SC2 character identifier containing region, program, realm,
    and a 64-bit toon ID. Profile storage uses a separate record address.</dd>

    <dt>Presence ID</dt>
    <dd>A connection-local native identifier for an online presence record. Local
    and master IDs can be aliases. Durable identity comes from the native account
    ID or toon handle.</dd>

    <dt>Member handle</dt>
    <dd>A channel-local Chat roster key. The same person can have a different member
    handle in every joined channel.</dd>

    <dt>LocatorKey</dt>
    <dd>A native Chat choice identifying a private, public, or club conference.
    Public channels use a locale FourCC and a channel-name string ID.</dd>

    <dt>Generated order</dt>
    <dd>The field order from the generated native serializer. It can differ from
    metadata order and contain filler bits. A separate declarative layout can
    describe field permutation and filler runs.</dd>

    <dt>Obfuscation marker</dt>
    <dd>Bit 6 in a metadata structural record. It indicates that generated handling
    may apply. A separate layout supplies field order and filler widths. Some marked
    types use ordinary reflection order.</dd>

    <dt>Resume</dt>
    <dd>The native Auth exchange that continues Front authorization on Sunken. It
    uses <code>Authentication::ResumeRequest</code> and
    <code>Authentication::ResumeResponse</code>. Sunken uses a new TCP connection
    and fresh native chat and game state.</dd>

    <dt>NO_RESPONSE</dt>
    <dd>A protobuf RPC declaration requiring omission of the reply frame.</dd>

    <dt>FourCC</dt>
    <dd>A four-byte symbolic identifier such as <code>Chat</code>, <code>Auth</code>,
    <code>Mc64</code>, or <code>enUS</code>.</dd>
  </dl>
